<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About the Weather</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Dress for the Weather.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Count up to 50 out loud with help. Interactive: Describe simple pictures or objects during classroom discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can count weather-related items up to 50 with assistance (e.g., counting "clouds" or "raindrops" in a picture). Can describe simple weather-related pictures or objects during classroom discussions (e.g., "This is a rainy day." "The umbrella is red."). Can respond to peer questions about weather and objects using simple phrases (e.g., "What is this?" "It's an umbrella.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on engaging students, activating prior knowledge, modeling target language, and clarifying concepts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying large weather flashcards (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and clothing flashcards (hat, coat, umbrella, boots). The teacher points to a weather card and asks, "What's the weather like today?" Students respond chorally or individually with simple words (e.g., "Sunny!", "Rainy!"). The teacher then points to a clothing item and asks, "What is this?" Students say the word. This activates vocabulary from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target sentences: "It's a sunny day. I wear a hat." "It's a rainy day. I wear a coat." The teacher repeats slowly, clearly articulating the /s/ sound in "sunny" and the /r/ sound in "rainy." The teacher also models the initial /w/ and final /i/ sounds in "windy." The teacher uses TPR, miming putting on clothes for each weather type. "I like sunny days."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions.
                    <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a child with a hat on a sunny day) "Is it cold?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of rain) "Do I need my umbrella?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage introduces new language content connected to the scenario, using authentic input and checking comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> "All About the Weather". The teacher presents a large, colorful picture poster showing different children dressed for various weather types (e.g., a child playing outside on a sunny day, a child jumping in puddles on a rainy day, a child with a scarf on a windy day). The poster also includes weather-related items such as clouds, raindrops, umbrellas, and hats, many of which are easily countable.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large illustrated poster titled "Our Weather Day." This poster features short, simple sentences describing the scenes (e.g., "It's a sunny day. I wear a hat. I play outside." "It's a rainy day. I wear a coat and boots."). The register is informal, designed for young learners.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The ability to describe the weather and activities and to express preferences using simple phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher points to a part of the poster and asks: "What is the weather like?" (Students respond: "Sunny!" "Rainy!"). "What do you see?" (Students respond: "An umbrella!" "A coat!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Point and Say:</b> The teacher says a word, e.g., "umbrella," and students point to the umbrella in the picture and repeat "umbrella." The teacher asks, "What color is the umbrella?" (Expected answer: "Red!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Counting:</b> The teacher points to a group of clouds or raindrops on the poster and asks, "How many clouds?" The teacher guides students to count chorally up to 10-20 at first, then individual students count up to 50 if prompted (e.g., "Count the raindrops for me, up to 50 if you can!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on accuracy through controlled and semi-controlled speaking practice, building confidence before freer production.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Speaking.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using adjectives + nouns ("rainy day"), Present simple ("I like sunny days."), WH-questions ("What is the weather like?"), and vocabulary related to weather and clothing. Practicing pronunciation of /s/, /r/, /w/, /i/. Counting up to 50.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Choral Repetition & Picture Description (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher shows weather and clothing flashcards. For each card, students repeat the weather type (e.g., "Sunny!", "Windy!") and then a full sentence: "It's a sunny day." The teacher then introduces clothing items: "I wear a hat." "I wear boots." The class repeats chorally. The teacher leads a counting exercise with flashcards that have multiple small items, e.g., a card with 30 small raindrops or 40 small clouds. "How many raindrops?" Students count together, "One, two, three... thirty!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Completion & Substitution Drill (Semi-Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides sentence frames on the board or orally. "It's a _____ day." (Teacher shows "cloudy" flashcard, students say "It's a cloudy day.") "I wear a _____." (Teacher shows "scarf" flashcard, students say "I wear a scarf."). The teacher uses a simple "What is the weather like?" prompt, and students respond using adjectives + nouns. The teacher highlights the /s/ sound in "sunny" and the /r/ sound in "rainy," and /w/ in "windy."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Q&A (Semi-Controlled Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of mixed weather and clothing flashcards. Student A picks a weather card and asks, "What's the weather like?" Student B responds, "It's a [sunny/rainy/cloudy/windy] day." Then Student B asks, "What do you wear?" Student A responds, "I wear a [hat/coat/umbrella/boots]." They swap roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation. Students are encouraged to use the discourse marker "and" (e.g., "It's sunny and warm.").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage involves students using the target language in a realistic, communicative task, focusing on fluency and collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "My Weather Outfit!"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a large picture of a person outline and various cut-out clothing items and accessories (hats, coats, scarves, boots, umbrellas, etc.) plus weather elements (suns, clouds, raindrops, wind lines). They also receive a simple weather scenario card (e.g., "It's a very rainy day.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group selects one weather scenario card.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They discuss (in simple English) what clothes the person should "wear" for that weather, and what weather elements to add to the picture. They glue the clothing items onto the person outline and the weather elements around them.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student from each group then presents their "weather outfit" to the class. The student points to their picture and says: "It's a [rainy/sunny/windy] day. I wear a [coat] and [boots]. I like [rainy days]." They also count a specific number of weather items on their poster (e.g., "Look, [25] raindrops!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other students can ask simple WH-questions like, "What is this?" (pointing to an item) or "What color is the [hat]?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in describing the weather, selecting appropriate clothing, and expressing preferences. Collaborative interaction within groups, using the target vocabulary and simple sentences. Encouraging the use of "and" for combining ideas. Counting up to 50 aloud with peer and teacher help if needed.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage provides formative assessment opportunities and allows students to reflect on their learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes students during their "My Weather Outfit!" presentations and group work, noting their ability to use target vocabulary, form simple sentences, and interact.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student presents their group's "Weather Outfit" picture. They describe the weather and what the person wears (e.g., "It's a sunny day, and I wear a hat. I like sunny days."). They also count a group of items (e.g., "I see 35 raindrops."). The teacher may ask one follow-up WH-question like "What is the weather like?" or "What color is the jacket?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of /s/ in "sunny," /r/ in "rainy," and initial /w/, final /i/ in "windy."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (weather nouns, clothing nouns, adjectives, verbs like "wear," "like").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of description:</b> Using simple sentences (Adjectives + nouns, Present simple) to describe the weather and clothing.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Counting:</b> Ability to count weather-related items up to 50 with assistance.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participating and contributing to the group discussion and presentation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Responding to questions:</b> Answering simple "What" questions from peers or teacher.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper with two faces (happy/sad) and two questions:
                    <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I talk about the weather and clothes?" (Students circle happy/sad face)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I count raindrops and clouds?" (Students circle happy/sad face)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage encourages both student and teacher reflection and connects the lesson to the upcoming learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new weather word did you learn today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun to talk about?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking-focused lesson? (e.g., the "My Weather Outfit!" activity generated good interaction and speaking, students were engaged in counting).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students struggled with numbers beyond 30, requiring more individual counting practice. Need to ensure more active participation from quieter students in group speaking tasks. Focus more on the "and" discourse marker).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will use the vocabulary and sentence structures practiced today to write simple sentences and short paragraphs about weather and appropriate clothing. For example, they will write sentences like "It's a sunny day. I wear a hat. I like sunny days and I play outside."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>